%S A399899 0,1,1,2,2,0,1,3,3,1,2,3,2,3,0,1,4,4,0,2,1,0,2,3,4,0,4,3,1,5,5,2,0,3,
%T A399899 4,1,2,4,3,5,0,5,4,6,6,1,4,2,5,3,6,5,6,0,1,2,3,4,0,6,0,5,6,4,0,5,1,0,
%U A399899 2,0,3,5,0,4,7,7,5,1,2,3,6,7,4,7,6,8,8
%N A399899 Word representation of the grasshopper infinite barrycade (recursively defined family of permutations of the positive integers having pairwise disjoint sets of partial sums).
%C A399899 A barrycade is a collection of rows whose sets of partial sums are pairwise disjoint. In the infinite version considered here, each row is a permutation of the positive integers. Let B_psi = {psi_1, psi_2, ...} be the grasshopper infinite barrycade defined in A399897, with each row regarded in its entirety as an infinite permutation. For n >= 1, a(n)=r if n occurs as a partial sum in row psi_r, and a(n)=0 if n does not occur as a partial sum in any row. Since the sets of partial sums of distinct rows are disjoint, a(n) is well-defined. Thus the sequence records, at every positive integer position, which row of the barrycade has a joint there; 0 marks a position missed by the whole barrycade.
%C A399899 The positions of the zeros are given by A399898, while the first occurrence of r is A399897(r).
%C A399899 The spelling "barrycade" is due to Richard Guy, who used it instead of "barricade" in honor of Barry Cipra, who prompted him to study the problem.
%H A399899 M. Dębski, J. Grytczuk, P. Naroski, B. Pawlik, J. Przybyło and M. Śleszyńska-Nowak, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18476">Finite and infinite barrycades</a>, arXiv:2609.18476 [math.CO], 2026.
%F A399899 a(n)=r if n belongs to S(psi_r), and a(n)=0 if n belongs to no S(psi_r), where S(psi_r) is the set of partial sums of row psi_r.
%F A399899 A399897(r) = min { n >= 1 : a(n)=r }; the positions of the zeros of a(n) form A399898.
%e A399899 The first three rows begin
%e A399899 psi1 = (2,1,4,3,6,5,...),
%e A399899 psi2 = (4,1,6,2,7,3,...),
%e A399899 psi_3 = (8,1,3,2,10,4,...).
%e A399899 Their partial sums begin respectively
%e A399899 2,3,7,10,16,21,...,
%e A399899 4,5,11,13,20,23,...,
%e A399899 8,9,12,14,24,28,...
%e A399899 Therefore positions 2,3,7,10 belong to row 1, positions 4,5 belong to row 2, positions 8,9 belong to row 3, while positions 1 and 6 are missed. Hence a(1)..a(10) = 0,1,1,2,2,0,1,3,3,1.
%t A399899 A399899[n_] := Module[{u,s,p,init,step,extend,covered,r=1,a=ConstantArray[0,n]},covered[0,_]=False;covered[j_,x_]:=AnyTrue[Range[j],(init[#];extend[#,x];MemberQ[p[#],x])&];step[j_]:=Module[{k=1,q=1},While[MemberQ[u[j],k],k++];While[MemberQ[u[j],q]||q==k||covered[j-1,s[j]+q]||covered[j-1,s[j]+q+k],q++];Scan[(AppendTo[u[j],#];s[j]+=#;AppendTo[p[j],s[j]])&,{q,k}]];init[j_]:=If[!ValueQ[s[j]],u[j]={};s[j]=0;p[j]={};step[j]];extend[j_,x_]:=(init[j];While[s[j]<x,step[j]]);While[True,init[r];If[First[u[r]]>n,Break[]];extend[r,n];Scan[If[#<=n,a[[#]]=r]&,p[r]];r++];a]
%Y A399899 Cf. A399897-A399909 for other sequences arising from infinite barrycade constructions.
%K A399899 nonn,new
%O A399899 1,4
%A A399899 _Bartlomiej Pawlik_, Sep 23 2026
